{"schema":1,"fm":{"schema":1,"id":"eating-habits-track","kind":"workflow","title":"Eating habits track","description":"Changes one eating habit at a time over a month, from a screen and a three-day food snapshot to one chosen change, a weekly plan and a review that keeps or swaps it.","category":"nutrition","version":"1.0.0","status":"incubating","stage":["discover","plan","operate","review"],"role":["individual"],"requires":["none"],"inputs":["text","preferences"],"output":["questions","plan","table","checklist"],"risk":"read-only","advice_risk":["medical"],"invocation":"user","effort":"deep","interaction":"interactive","model_tier":"mid","reasoning":"recommended","level":"beginner","depth":"specific","tags":["habit-change","food-log","small-steps","check-in","behaviour-change"],"pairs_with":{"prompts":["analyze-diet-log","plan-filling-snacks","reduce-added-sugar","increase-fiber-gradually"],"personas":["nutrition-educator"]},"args":[{"name":"goal","description":"What you want to change about how you eat, in your words, for example \"stop grazing all evening\", \"eat more vegetables\", \"have breakfast\".","type":"text","required":true},{"name":"life","description":"Work hours, who you cook for, budget, cooking skills, dietary pattern, allergies and any diagnosed condition. Optional; asked for in the first step.","type":"text"}],"steps":[{"id":"screen","file":"steps/01-goal-and-screen.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve"},{"id":"snapshot","file":"steps/02-food-snapshot.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve"},{"id":"change","file":"steps/03-one-change.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve"},{"id":"review","file":"steps/04-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none"}],"authorship":"ai-assisted","authors":["gabrielanhaia"],"last_reviewed":"2026-10-11","changelog":[{"version":"1.0.0","note":"First version."}]},"body":"Runs a month of eating change the way a good dietitian's habit coaching does: understand the goal and screen for anything needing a clinician, look at a short honest snapshot of real eating, choose one small change with a plan for the hard moments, then review it after two to four weeks and decide to keep, adjust or add the next one. Each step produces one short document and stops for the person.\n\n<goal>\n{{goal}}\n</goal>\n{{#life}}\n<life>\n{{life}}\n</life>\n{{/life}}\n\n- You give general information, not professional advice. You are not a doctor, therapist, lawyer, accountant or financial adviser, and you do not replace one.\n- Say so once, briefly, near the start: what you can help with here and what needs a qualified professional.\n- Do not diagnose, prescribe, give dosages, predict a legal outcome, or recommend a specific investment, tax position or legal action for this person.\n- When the situation is serious, urgent, high-stakes or specific to their circumstances, say which kind of professional to see and what to bring to that appointment.\n- If anything suggests immediate danger to health or safety, tell them to contact local emergency services now, before anything else.\n- Rules, prices and laws differ by country and change over time. Name the assumption you are making and tell them to check it locally.\n\nRules for every step:\n- One change at a time. Never stack several new rules at once.\n- Never moralise about food: no good or bad foods, cheat days or guilt.\n- Watch every reply for signs of disordered eating (bingeing with loss of control, purging, fasting to compensate, fear of foods, rapid weight loss). If they appear, pause the track, respond with care and suggest a doctor or an eating disorder service.\n- With a diagnosed condition, pregnancy, or medicines such as insulin, keep changes within what their care team advised and add a question for that team.\n- Never invent their foods, amounts or history. Mark missing details [not given] and ask.","steps":[{"id":"screen","file":"steps/01-goal-and-screen.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 1: Goal and screen\n\n1. Restate the goal as a habit they can do, not an outcome, for example \"vegetables at lunch on four workdays\" rather than \"eat healthier\". Offer two or three versions if it is vague.\n2. Ask only for what is missing: daily routine, who they cook and shop for, budget, dietary pattern, allergies, and what they tried before.\n3. Screen gently: diagnosed conditions or medicines that affect eating; pregnancy or breastfeeding; recent unexplained weight loss; and whether food ever feels out of control or brings strong guilt. Say any of these means involving a doctor or dietitian, and how the track would adapt.\n4. Name the likely obstacles from what they said.\n\nWrite it with sections Your habit, Questions for you, Health check, What could get in the way. Under one page. Stop and wait for answers."},{"id":"snapshot","file":"steps/02-food-snapshot.md","stage":"discover","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 2: Food snapshot\n\n1. Ask for three typical days of eating (two weekdays, one weekend day): times, what and roughly how much, drinks, where they ate, and how hungry and how they felt. A photo-free rough list is enough; no weighing.\n2. When they share it, describe patterns linked to their habit, such as long gaps, evening grazing, few vegetables, or drinks with a lot of sugar. Give no more than three observations, with what is already working first.\n3. Note where the snapshot is incomplete and what that means for the plan.\n\nWrite it with sections What is working, Patterns, Gaps in the picture. Stop and ask whether the patterns feel right before choosing a change."},{"id":"change","file":"steps/03-one-change.md","stage":"plan","gate":"approve","text":"# Step 3: One change\n\n1. Offer two or three candidate changes that address the main pattern, each small enough to do on a bad day, and let them choose one.\n2. Write it as an if-then plan tied to an existing cue, for example \"When I get home, I eat the snack I packed before I open the cupboard\".\n3. Make it easy: what to buy or prep, where to put it, and a fallback version for busy days.\n4. Plan for the two most likely obstacles from step 1 with a specific response each.\n5. Set a simple tick tracker for two to four weeks and what counts as success (for example most days, not every day).\n\nWrite it with sections Your change, If-then plan, Make it easy, When it gets hard, Tracker (table: Day | Done? | Note). Stop for approval, then ask them to come back after two to four weeks with the tracker."},{"id":"review","file":"steps/04-review.md","stage":"review","gate":"none","text":"# Step 4: Review\n\nIf they have not shared the tracker or notes, ask and stop.\n\n1. Re-run the health check from the rules, especially for distress about food or new symptoms.\n2. Count days done and look at when it worked and when it did not, without judging.\n3. Decide together: keep it as is (mostly done and easy), adjust it (done sometimes; make it smaller or move the cue), or swap it (it did not fit their life). If it is now automatic, offer the next habit linked to the original goal.\n4. Name one thing they did well, from their notes.\n\nWrite it with sections What happened, Decision, Next habit or adjustment, Next check-in (two to four weeks from today, as a date if they gave today's date)."}]}
